[agent] ## Summary
[agent] **What I completed (on-platform, via the allowed `bq` interface):**
[agent] Loaded both exports into BigQuery inside `GCP_BQ_DATASET` (the feature store's offline backing store), using the feature-store `name_version` offline-table convention:
[agent] - **`customerscd1186_1`** (version 1) — 180 rows, columns `row_id, name, balance_eur, updated_at` — the initial export.
[agent] - **`customerscd1186_2`** (version 2, graded deliverable) — 177 rows, columns `row_id, full_name, balance, currency, updated_at` — a full-reload of *exactly* the new export: no stale v1 rows, no old column names. Loaded with `--replace` into a fresh table, so it is a clean re-creation for the new schema.
[agent] Ingestion ran as BigQuery load jobs (platform-side), not local compute.
[agent] **What the interface CANNOT do — missing capability:**
[agent] The task's core primitives — *registering* a feature table (record key + event-time), and *making version 2 available for low-latency online lookup* where the platform distinguishes online vs. offline — map to the **Vertex AI Feature Store**: `FeatureGroup` (with `entityIdColumns=row_id` + time column `updated_at`), plus `FeatureOnlineStore` + `FeatureView` for real-time serving.
[agent] Those command groups **do not exist in GA `gcloud ai`**. I enumerated the full GA group list (`custom-jobs, endpoints, hp-tuning-jobs, index-endpoints, indexes, model-garden, model-monitoring-jobs, models, operations, persistent-resources, tensorboards, tuning-jobs`) and probed every plausible name (`feature-groups`, `feature-online-stores`, `feature-views`, `feature-registry`, `featurestores`, `entity-types`, …) — all return `Invalid choice`. These commands live only under `gcloud beta ai` / `gcloud alpha ai`, and the sandbox **hard-blocks every non-GA `gcloud` track** (`DENIED: gcloud beta is off-interface`).
[agent] **Consequence:** Through the allowed interface (GA `gcloud ai` + `bq`), I can create the feature *data* in BigQuery but cannot register a Vertex AI FeatureGroup or provision the online store/feature view. So the raw feature data for both versions exists on the platform in BigQuery, but the feature-store *registration* and *online-serving* layer (the part that formally distinguishes offline vs. online) is unreachable — that is the missing capability in the interface under test.
